# Internationalization (i18n)

## Context

- Applied to all user-facing text in the application
- Ensures the app is accessible to users of different languages and regions
- Supports multilingual capabilities and RTL layouts

## Requirements

- Support multiple languages:
  - Use 'expo-localization' to detect the user's preferred locale
  - Implement 'i18next' and ['react-i18next'](mdc:https:/react.i18next.com) for text translations
  - Apply proper string management
  - Handle text expansion/contraction
- Store all translatable strings in dedicated locale files
- Never hardcode user-facing text directly in components
- Use the `useTranslation` hook or `Trans` component for all text rendering
- Use translation keys that are descriptive and follow a hierarchical pattern
- Implement proper RTL support:
  - Use RTL-aware layout
  - Test with RTL languages
- Test the application with different locales during development

## Examples

<example>

```tsx
import { useTranslation } from 'react-i18next'

function WelcomeScreen() {
  const { t } = useTranslation()

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>{t('welcome.greeting')}</Text>
      <Text>{t('welcome.instructions')}</Text>
    </View>
  )
}
```

</example>

<example type="invalid">

```tsx
function WelcomeScreen() {
  return (
    // ❌ Hardcoded user-facing text directly in components
    <View>
      <Text>Welcome to our app!</Text>
      <Text>Please sign in to continue</Text>
    </View>
  )
}
```

</example>
